# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Esther'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'history'
SUBTITLE = 'The Jews Scattered, the Decree Reversed, and the Feast of Purim'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Esther is set in the reign of Ahasuerus over “an hundred and seven and twenty provinces” (Esth. 1:1). Esther, a Jewess brought up by her kinsman Mordecai, becomes queen; Haman seeks to destroy “all the Jews that were throughout the whole kingdom”; the king’s decree is answered by fasting, by Esther’s going in to the king, and by a second decree; and the days of Purim are kept (Esth. 2:7, 17; 3:6; 4:16; 9:26).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where Israel is a people scattered among the nations yet preserved, with Mordecai the Jew and Esther the queen standing among them (Esth. 2:5; 3:8; 10:3).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Esther', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Feast and the New Queen (Chs. 1–2)'), ('p', 'Vashti is set aside, Esther is brought up and made queen, and Mordecai sits at the king’s gate (Esth. 1:1; 2:7, 17).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Haman’s Decree (Chs. 3–4)'), ('p', 'Haman, offended by Mordecai, obtains a decree against the Jews; Mordecai mourns and sends word to Esther (Esth. 3:6, 13; 4:1, 14).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — Esther Goes In (Chs. 4–7)'), ('p', 'The Jews fast three days, Esther goes in to the king, and Haman is exposed at her banquet (Esth. 4:16; 7:6).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Decree Reversed (Chs. 8–9)'), ('p', 'A second decree is sent, the Jews have joy and gladness, and their enemies’ hope is turned (Esth. 8:17; 9:1).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — Purim Kept (Chs. 9–10)'), ('p', 'Mordecai writes to the Jews, the days of Purim are set, and Mordecai is next unto the king (Esth. 9:20, 26; 10:3).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Esther', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>A People Scattered</b> — Haman speaks of “a certain people scattered abroad and dispersed among the people in all the provinces of thy kingdom” (Esth. 3:8).', '<b>Deliverance from Another Place</b> — “Then shall there enlargement and deliverance arise to the Jews from another place” (Esth. 4:14).', '<b>Fasting and Courage</b> — “Go, gather together all the Jews that are present in Shushan, and fast ye for me” (Esth. 4:16).', '<b>The Adversary Exposed</b> — “The adversary and enemy is this wicked Haman” (Esth. 7:6).', '<b>Sorrow Turned to Joy</b> — The Jews “rested from their enemies, and the month which was turned unto them from sorrow to joy” (Esth. 9:22).', '<b>A Memorial in Every Generation</b> — “These days should be remembered and kept throughout every generation, every family, every province, and every city” (Esth. 9:28).', '<b>Mordecai Seeking His People’s Good</b> — Mordecai was “seeking the wealth of his people, and speaking peace to all his seed” (Esth. 10:3).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Esther', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Esther points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Preserved People</b> — The book follows the Jews as a people kept alive in exile, from whom Christ comes after the flesh; the book itself names no such line and is reported as written (Esth. 2:5; 9:28).', '<b>Deliverance and Reversal</b> — A sentence of death is turned to joy for the Jews (Esth. 9:22). The book is read as the preservation of Israel and is not applied to the Body.', '<b>The Hidden Hand</b> — The book does not name God, and it is reported as written; the deliverance is told in what happened and in Mordecai’s words (Esth. 4:14).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Esther', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Esther speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Esther operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Esther in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Esther reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['Jewish identity among the nations (Esth. 2:5; 3:8)', 'Esther and Mordecai as the Jews’ deliverers (Esth. 4:14; 10:3)', 'a decree turned against those who sought their destruction (Esth. 7:6; 9:1)', 'the memorial days of Purim for their seed (Esth. 9:26, 28)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Esther that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Esther contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Jew in Shushan — “a certain Jew, whose name was Mordecai, the son of Jair, the son of Shimei, the son of Kish, a Benjamite.” (Esth. 2:5).', 'Esther Made Queen — “the king loved Esther above all the women… and made her queen instead of Vashti.” (Esth. 2:17).', 'A People Scattered — “a certain people scattered abroad and dispersed among the people in all the provinces of thy kingdom.” (Esth. 3:8).', 'Deliverance to the Jews — “then shall there enlargement and deliverance arise to the Jews from another place.” (Esth. 4:14).', 'The Fast of the Jews — “gather together all the Jews that are present in Shushan, and fast ye for me.” (Esth. 4:16).', 'The Enemy Named — “The adversary and enemy is this wicked Haman.” (Esth. 7:6).', 'Joy and Gladness — “the Jews had joy and gladness, a feast and a good day.” (Esth. 8:17).', 'Sorrow Turned to Joy — “the month which was turned unto them from sorrow to joy.” (Esth. 9:22).', 'The Days of Purim — “they called these days Purim after the name of Pur.” (Esth. 9:26).', 'Remembered Every Generation — “these days should be remembered and kept throughout every generation.” (Esth. 9:28).', 'Mordecai the Jew — “Mordecai the Jew was next unto king Ahasuerus, and great among the Jews.” (Esth. 10:3).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Esther Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Esther and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Esther teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Esther', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Jew in Shushan', '“a certain Jew, whose name was Mordecai, the son of Jair, the son of Shimei, the son of Kish, a Benjamite.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 2:5'], ['Esther Made Queen', '“the king loved Esther above all the women… and made her queen instead of Vashti.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 2:17'], ['A People Scattered', '“a certain people scattered abroad and dispersed among the people in all the provinces of thy kingdom.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 3:8'], ['Deliverance to the Jews', '“then shall there enlargement and deliverance arise to the Jews from another place.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 4:14'], ['The Fast of the Jews', '“gather together all the Jews that are present in Shushan, and fast ye for me.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 4:16'], ['The Enemy Named', '“The adversary and enemy is this wicked Haman.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 7:6'], ['Joy and Gladness', '“the Jews had joy and gladness, a feast and a good day.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 8:17'], ['Sorrow Turned to Joy', '“the month which was turned unto them from sorrow to joy.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 9:22'], ['The Days of Purim', '“they called these days Purim after the name of Pur.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 9:26'], ['Remembered Every Generation', '“these days should be remembered and kept throughout every generation.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 9:28'], ['Mordecai the Jew', '“Mordecai the Jew was next unto king Ahasuerus, and great among the Jews.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Esth. 10:3']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Esther that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Esther teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Courage in a Hard Hour', '“I also and my maidens will fast likewise; and so will I go in unto the king.”', 'Esth. 4:16'], ['Mourning Before God', '“Mordecai rent his clothes, and put on sackcloth with ashes.”', 'Esth. 4:1'], ['Speaking Peace', '“seeking the wealth of his people, and speaking peace to all his seed.”', 'Esth. 10:3']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Esther without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Esther in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Take courage in a hard hour</b> — Esther goes in at the risk of her life (Esth. 4:16).', '<b>Mourn honestly</b> — Mordecai cries with a loud and bitter cry (Esth. 4:1).', '<b>Seek others’ good</b> — Mordecai speaks peace to all his seed (Esth. 10:3).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Esther', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Esther in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Feast and the New Queen (Chs. 1–2) — Esther raised up. <i>(Esth. 1:1; 2:7, 17)</i>', 'Haman’s Decree (Chs. 3–4) — A people marked for destruction. <i>(Esth. 3:6, 8; 4:14)</i>', 'Esther Goes In (Chs. 4–7) — The fast and the banquet. <i>(Esth. 4:16; 7:6)</i>', 'The Decree Reversed (Chs. 8–9) — Joy for the Jews. <i>(Esth. 8:17; 9:22)</i>', 'Purim Kept (Chs. 9–10) — The memorial and Mordecai. <i>(Esth. 9:26, 28; 10:3)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Esther, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward devotion, courage, and trust in unseen providence without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Esther simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Esther stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]

# Place in God's Storyline (Master Standard v1.155): last item of Section 1
